SELECTING ELEMETS WITH JS

document.getElementsByTagName('li');
It get backs the arrays, SELECTS ALL OF THE ELEMENTS 


document.getElementsByTagName('li')[2].style.color = 'purple';

document.getElementsByTagName('li').length;

document.getElementsByClassName('btn');
Gives you an array


document.getElementById("title").innerHTML = "Hello World!";

document.querySelector('h1');
<h1>​Hello​</h1>​


https://www.w3schools.com/jsref/dom_obj_style.asp



MANIPULTE CSS PROPERTY WITH JS

﻿
	• document.querySelector('h1').style.color='blue';
	'blue'
	• document.querySelector('h1').style.fontSize='10rem';
	'10rem'
	• document.querySelector('h1').style.padding='30%';
	'30%'
	
	


Html -> CONTENT ONLY
CSS -> CSS
Js -> behaviour

MANIPULATION OF HTML WITH JS
document.querySelector('h1').innerHTML="<em> Hola</em>";

MANIPULATION OF HTML FOR ATRIBUTTES 
document.querySelector('a');
#<a href="http://www.google.com>Google</a>"

MANIPULATION OF HTML FOR ATRIBUTTES 
document.querySelector('a').attributes;
NamedNodeMap {0: href, href: href, length: 1}


MANIPULATION OF HTML FOR ATRIBUTTES 
document.querySelector('a').getAttribute("href");
'https://www.google.com'

MANIPULATION OF HTML FOR ATRIBUTTES 
document.querySelector('a').setAttribute("href", "https://www.bing.com");


HIGHER ORDER FUNCTIONS AND PASSING FUNCTIONS AS ARGUMENTS

$0.addEventListener(event happening, function);

Example

$0.addEventListener("click", respondToClick)

function respondToClick() {
	console.log("I got clicked");
}

FUNCTION INSIDE A FUNCTION
function add(num1, num2) {
	return num1 + num2;
	}
	
function substract(num1, num2) {
	return num1 - num2;
	}


function multiply(num1, num2) {
	return num1 * num2;
	}

function divide(num1, num2) {
	return num1 / num2;
	}


	
function calculator (num1, num2, operator) {
	return operator(num1, num2);
	}


